WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:18.930
Karl Benz - Father of the Automobile
Looking back on humanity’s achievements,

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:23.690
you can pinpoint certain landmark moments
that changed the world forever.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:28.640
One such moment was, undoubtedly, the birth
of the automobile which, like rail transport

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.300
before it and aviation afterwards, completely
revolutionized how humans moved around.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.520
Many people deserve credit for this achievement.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.881
As you are about to find out, the concept
of the “first car in the world” is, by

00:00:39.881 --> 00:00:46.180
no means, a settled matter, but the lion’s
share of the accolades goes to Karl Benz who

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:51.360
designed and built what is regarded by many
as the true first automobile.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.980
They say that behind every great man, there
is a great woman and, perhaps, there is no

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.730
better example than Bertha Benz.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:02.800
She was there to support her husband through
thick and thin, enjoying the highs together

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.380
and using her own money to get him back on
his feet during the lows.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:11.890
Then, of course, there was also her iconic
road trip which solidified the car as a practical

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:12.890
machine.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:17.689
Bertha’s daringness combined with Karl’s
engineering genius ensured that people finally

00:01:17.689 --> 00:01:22.119
looked at the car as something that could
actually be used in day-to-day life.

00:01:22.119 --> 00:01:27.630
We’ve come a long way since then, but it
would be completely fair to say that the automotive

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:34.399
industry would not be what it is today without
Karl Benz.

00:01:34.399 --> 00:01:41.719
Early Years
Karl Benz, sometimes spelled with a C instead

00:01:41.719 --> 00:01:49.389
of a K, was born Karl Friedrich Vaillant on
November 25, 1844, in the German town of Mühlburg

00:01:49.389 --> 00:01:54.899
which today is a borough of Karlsruhe in the
state of Baden-Württemberg.

00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:59.869
His name was Vaillant because his father,
Johann Georg Benz, and his mother, Josephine

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:02.799
Vaillant, were not married at the time of
his birth.

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:08.549
However, they did, in fact, marry a few months
later and the baby became Karl Friedrich Benz.

00:02:08.549 --> 00:02:13.581
Johann Benz was a locomotive driver, but he
died in an accident when Karl was only two

00:02:13.581 --> 00:02:14.581
years old.

00:02:14.581 --> 00:02:19.290
This left the family in dire financial straits,
but the mother did her best to ensure a good

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.860
education for her son.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:25.239
Young Karl followed in his father’s footsteps
and studied engineering and, from an early

00:02:25.239 --> 00:02:27.230
age, proved to be a prodigious student.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:32.099
In fact, when he was just 19 years old, Karl
Benz graduated with a degree in mechanical

00:02:32.099 --> 00:02:35.150
engineering from the University of Karlsruhe.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:40.629
This was in 1864 and yet, it would be a while
before Benz finally found his vocation in

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:41.629
life.

00:02:41.629 --> 00:02:45.590
He spent the next seven years trying out various
jobs with multiple construction and engineering

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:49.299
firms throughout Germany, but he found them
all to be unsatisfying.

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:54.480
At the same time, he became an avid bicycle
rider and enjoyed tinkering with them, fantasizing

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.290
about a similar vehicle that would be motorized.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.299
In 1871, Karl Benz started his first business.

00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:04.860
He partnered with a mechanic named August
Ritter and opened the Iron Foundry and Mechanical

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.400
Workshop in Mannheim.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.829
The company was not successful and, within
a year, it was struggling financially.

00:03:10.829 --> 00:03:15.489
Fortunately for Benz, he soon found a better
partner, one who would have an enormous impact

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:23.560
on his life and the development of the automobile
- his bride-to-be, Bertha Ringer.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:30.370
Karl and Bertha
Bertha Ringer had been born in a time when

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:35.480
it was firmly believed that women had no business
with scientific and mechanical matters and

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:40.299
that even just exerting their brains would
decrease their ability to have children.

00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:44.430
Certainly not the case here - Karl and Bertha
would go on to have five children together.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:48.080
And yet, even when she was young, Bertha took
an interest in engineering.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.239
According to a particularly heartbreaking
legend, she became determined to show the

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:56.499
world that women were capable of achieving
great things after reading her family’s

00:03:56.499 --> 00:04:01.739
Bible one day and finding an entry that said
“Unfortunately only a girl again” - written

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:04.359
by her father, on the day she was born.

00:04:04.359 --> 00:04:07.980
Whether this story is true or not, the elder
Mr. Ringer did leave Bertha a substantial

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:11.579
dowry after working as a successful carpenter
all his life.

00:04:11.579 --> 00:04:15.819
Besides her wealth, Bertha Ringer was also
a beautiful and clearly intelligent woman.

00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:19.970
When she reached marrying age, she had a long
list of potential suitors to choose from.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:25.060
And yet, fate led to a poor, disheveled engineer
named Karl Benz to share the same coach as

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.090
Bertha and her mother during an excursion
in 1869.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:32.810
The two started talking and, as soon as Karl
brought up a horseless carriage he was working

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:36.960
on, he captured Bertha’s interest and, soon
enough, her heart.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.000
Despite warnings and admonishments from her
family, Bertha’s mind was made up - Karl

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.030
was the man she wanted to be with for the
rest of her life.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.759
The couple married in 1872.

00:04:45.759 --> 00:04:48.310
Bertha’s support of her husband was unwavering.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:53.120
Her first act was to use part of her dowry
to buy out August Ritter’s share in Benz’s

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.120
company.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.280
The rest of her money was used to keep the
business afloat while Karl worked on new patents.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:03.810
Although Benz was, undoubtedly, a genius engineer
and designer, nobody could ever accuse him

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:08.199
of being a shrewd businessman.

00:05:08.199 --> 00:05:14.130
The Benz Companies
Benz’s first great achievement was his stationary

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.000
gasoline two-stroke engine.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.840
He was inspired by the work of an earlier
German engineer, Nikolaus Otto, who created

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:23.180
the first four-stroke engine in 1861.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:28.590
Benz received a patent for his invention in
1880 and went on to add improvements such

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:33.740
as an engine speed regulation system and ignition
via a battery system.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:38.129
This new device of Benz managed to garner
interest from banks and businessmen who wanted

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:39.270
to invest.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.979
Because he needed the capital to grow his
company, Benz converted his business into

00:05:42.979 --> 00:05:48.190
a joint-stock company named Gasmotoren-Fabrik
Mannheim in 1882.

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:50.649
This did not work out as he hoped it would.

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:54.750
After all the agreements were completed, Benz
ended up only owning five percent of the company.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:58.159
More importantly, he did not have a say in
how the business was run, anymore.

00:05:58.159 --> 00:05:59.159
Not a significant one, at least.

00:05:59.159 --> 00:06:03.509
His partners had differing notions on what
products to design and sell and the engineer’s

00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:05.080
ideas often went ignored.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:08.909
Not happy with this arrangement, Karl Benz
left the corporation the following year.

00:06:08.909 --> 00:06:14.250
Undeterred, Benz looked to start a new company
but, this time, he searched for more amenable

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:15.250
partners.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:20.099
He found them in Max Rose and Friedrich Wilhelm
Esslinger, two men who owned a bicycle repair

00:06:20.099 --> 00:06:21.229
shop in Mannheim.

00:06:21.229 --> 00:06:26.050
Together, they established the Benz &amp; Companie
Rheinische Gasmotoren-Fabrik, better known

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:27.710
simply as Benz &amp; Co.

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:32.300
The business focused on manufacturing engines
and proved to be profitable, growing to 25

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:34.240
employees in a short amount of time.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:39.010
This newfound success eased the pressure off
Benz a little bit and allowed him to indulge

00:06:39.010 --> 00:08:01.199
in his passion project - building an automobile
using

00:08:01.199 --> 00:08:31.930
his engine.

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:48.960
The First Car?

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.730
In 1885, Karl Benz finished his magnum opus
- the Benz Patent Motorwagen.

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:54.940
It was a two-seater vehicle.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:09:00.760
It had three wire-spoke wheels and a single-cylinder,
gasoline-powered engine that formed a single

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:07.060
unit with the chassis and was capable of producing
0.75 horsepower and reaching top speeds of

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:08.060
10 mph.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:12.880
Other bells and whistles on the vehicle included
a controlled exhaust valve, an automatic intake

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.180
slide, and high-voltage electrical vibrator
ignition with spark plug.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:23.920
In January of 1886, Benz received patent number
37435 for his “vehicle powered by a gas

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:28.670
engine” or the “birth certificate of the
automobile”, as it came to be known.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:31.770
Later in July, Benz drove his car in public
for the first time.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:34.230
So was this the first car in the world?

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:39.330
Well, for the sake of simplicity, it is generally
regarded as the first automobile, but that

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:45.420
question is difficult to answer because it
depends on what your definition of an “automobile”

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:46.420
is.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:50.350
The Motorwagen definitely does not look like
our idea of a “car” today, but it was

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:54.550
the first vehicle of its kind to be made commercially
available to the public.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:59.250
Benz’s machine had another advantage over
designs that came before it in that it managed

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:05.040
to function without exploding or falling apart
while in operation.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:10.000
The truth is that some people regard an automobile
as simply a self-propelled vehicle powered

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:14.940
by some kind of engine and, in that case,
we have to go even farther back than horseless

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:16.120
carriages.

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:21.100
French inventor Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot created
a full-size mechanical land vehicle called

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:24.310
the Fardier à vapeur back in 1769.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:28.580
It weighed over 8,800 lbs and was powered
by steam.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:33.730
In 1807, another Frenchman named Isaac de
Rivaz designed an experimental vehicle powered

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:35.210
by hydrogen gas.

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:39.270
Robert Anderson was a Scottish inventor who
created an electric carriage sometime during

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:43.310
the 1830s which was powered by non-rechargeable
batteries.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:49.580
In 1867, Belgian Jean-Joseph-Etienne Lenoir
built a vehicle powered by an atmospheric

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:50.940
gas engine.

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:55.090
German Siegfried Marcus did something similar
a few years later by combining a handcart

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:56.560
with an atmospheric engine.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:00.750
He did keep improving his design and ended
up with something that could be called a car,

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:04.500
but this happened in 1888, after the Benz
Patent Motorwagen.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:08.440
We should also mention the French team of
Édouard Delamare-Deboutteville and his assistant

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:13.740
Leon Malandin who started work on a vehicle
with an internal combustion engine and a three-wheeled

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:15.080
chassis back in 1883.

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:20.190
Some people do argue that Delamare-Deboutteville
might deserve some of the accolades usually

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:24.450
given to Karl Benz, but the truth of the matter
is that his vehicle never functioned well

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:25.830
enough to go into production.

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:30.860
Last, but not least, we should also include
Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach who were

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:35.550
working on their own automobile independently
of Karl Benz, at the same time, about 60 miles

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:36.550
away.

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:40.520
They finished the Daimler Motorcoach a couple
of months after the Benz Motorwagen.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:44.320
As you can see, the question of “Who built
the first car?” is a tricky one and does

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:50.300
not have a universal answer, although we can
generally agree that the Benz Patent Motorwagen

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:56.250
was the first practical automobile and, therefore,
set in motion the nascent stages of an industry

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:59.650
that would grow to become one of the biggest
in the world.

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:07.430
The Trip that Changed Everything
After the original Motorwagen came out, Benz

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:08.880
immediately went back to work.

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:12.860
Two new and improved models of his automobile
were released in the next couple of years.

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:17.790
But it seemed like the rest of the world did
not share Karl and Bertha’s vision for an

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:21.830
automotive future as demand for the Motorwagen
was not particularly high.

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:26.770
The Benz engine still sold nicely, but what
would be the point of buying a whole vehicle?

00:12:26.770 --> 00:12:31.510
Up until then, these kinds of self-powered
machines were only used in trials over short

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:34.350
distances and still needed mechanics to be
on-hand.

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.340
There was no practicality to them whatsoever.

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:41.490
Not to mention the fact that they were expensive
and would have only been accessible to the

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:42.490
rich elite.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:46.110
They were not interested, though, because
they considered something like the Motorwagen

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:49.850
to be too loud and messy - not very gentlemanly.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:53.100
What the car needed was a bit of good PR.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:56.890
Like we said before, Karl Benz was at his
best when he was getting his hands dirty in

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:57.890
the workshop.

00:12:57.890 --> 00:13:01.860
Fortunately for him, Bertha had a better mind
for marketing and she realized that they needed

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:07.390
some kind of stunt not only to garner publicity
for their new product, but also to show people

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:08.870
that it had a purpose.

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:13.040
Therefore, in the early hours of a hot August
day in 1888, Bertha Benz jumped behind the

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:18.150
wheel of a Benz Patent Motorwagen No. 3 and
embarked on one of the most important automotive

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:22.850
journeys in history - the first long-distance
trip by car.

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:25.350
Bertha did not tell her husband of her plan.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:29.520
She also did not inform the authorities so,
technically, what she did was illegal.

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:33.850
Bertha recruited her teenage sons, Richard
and Eugen, to tag along for the ride, and

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:38.830
left Mannheim in the morning on the pretense
of going to visit her mother in Pforzheim.

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:44.080
This was 66 miles away and, while that might
sound like a routine trip nowadays, it was

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:49.080
significantly longer than any journey that
had been previously attempted in any kind

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:50.260
of motorized carriage.

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:54.650
Moreover, Bertha had to contend with problems
that nobody else had to deal with, starting

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:58.220
with the fact that there were no roads suitable
for cars back then.

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:02.500
She had to follow wagon tracks for most of
the way and even went on ancient Roman roads

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:03.950
in some parts.

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:08.870
Another problem was fuel as the Motorwagen
needed to refuel every 15 to 20 miles and,

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:11.460
obviously, there were no gas stations anywhere.

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:16.140
Her automobile used a fuel called ligroin
which was a petroleum-based solvent that could

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:17.350
be bought at pharmacies.

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:21.980
Bertha made a stop at Wiesloch and paid a
visit to the local apothecary, looking to

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:23.870
buy ten liters of ligroin.

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:27.780
This startled the elderly chemist who saw
a young woman with stains on her dress and

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:31.690
naturally assumed that she wanted to use the
solvent to clean her clothes.

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:35.400
He offered her a one-liter bottle, noting
that that would be sufficient but Bertha,

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:36.400
of course, had no interest in clean clothes.

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:37.830
She was looking to refuel her car.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:41.630
In the end, she purchased three liters of
ligroin which was all the chemist had in stock

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:45.990
but, since then, that pharmacy in Wiesloch
has branded itself the first fuel station

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:46.990
in the world.

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:50.000
It even has a monument of Bertha and her two
sons in front of it.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:54.030
Other problems included needing to refill
the water tank regularly, a chain that broke

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:58.170
and had to be fixed by a blacksmith, the leather
brake pads that wore out and had to be replaced

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:02.330
by shoemakers, and even the occasional steep
hill where the boys had to get out and push

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:03.420
the car.

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:08.210
Bertha even showed off her own know-how on
occasion, using her garter to plug a leak

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:10.480
and hairpins to clear up clogged valves.

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:15.880
The 66-mile journey took over 12 hours, but
Bertha, Richard, and Eugen made it to Pforzheim

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:16.880
safely.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:19.060
More importantly, the trip achieved its goal.

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:23.200
Many people took notice, some were even frightened
to the point of prayer by the crazy woman

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:26.690
speeding through their towns in a “smoking
monster.”

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:30.340
Almost overnight, everyone heard of the Benz
Patent Motorwagen.

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:34.281
And in case they missed her the first time,
they had another chance soon after because,

00:15:34.281 --> 00:15:39.880
a few days later, Bertha and the boys returned
home in the same car, but on a different route.

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:43.940
Moreover, Bertha even identified a few ways
of improving the Benz car, such as adding

00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:46.280
a new gear for climbing hills.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:51.760
In 2008, this 121-mile long return-trip from
Mannheim to Pforzheim was declared a European

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:55.990
Route of Industrial Heritage and there is
a parade of antique cars that follows it every

00:15:55.990 --> 00:16:00.820
two years in celebration of Bertha’s iconic
voyage.

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:07.800
Finding Success
Bertha’s trip was a turning point for the

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.750
automobile industry, but especially for the
Benz company.

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:15.830
Of course, we were still decades away from
the car becoming the standard method of transportation,

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:20.380
but people started to see it not as some kind
of oddity, but the way of the future.

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:25.940
The Benz Patent Motorwagen went on sale in
1888 and, bizarrely, it sold better in France

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:26.940
than in Germany.

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:30.140
Again, this was down to Karl Benz’s lack
of business acumen.

00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:33.300
Because of this, transactions were slow in
his native country.

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:38.110
In France, however, the marketing was done
by a Parisian engineer and bicycle manufacturer

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:41.630
named Emile Roger who had more of a flair
for showmanship.

00:16:41.630 --> 00:16:46.620
Roger was already a successful distributor
of Benz engines and he received sole agency

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:49.310
rights to sell Benz cars in France.

00:16:49.310 --> 00:16:54.160
Some of his creative marketing ideas included
showing off his Motorwagen Model III at the

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:58.220
1889 World’s Fair and signing up the cars
for racing events.

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:02.210
There was some tension between the two because
Roger made an effort to minimize Benz’s

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:05.500
role in the development of the car and increase
his own.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:10.000
He gave out leaflets to prospective buyers
that described the automobile, not once mentioning

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:11.309
the name “Benz.”

00:17:11.309 --> 00:17:16.459
He made special Roger badges that he fitted
to the cars he sold and he made sure that

00:17:16.459 --> 00:17:20.509
the vehicles he raced were all referred to
as Roger cars.

00:17:20.509 --> 00:17:24.519
Even with Roger’s shenanigans, the Benz
company continued to grow and had to relocate

00:17:24.519 --> 00:17:26.209
to a bigger production factory.

00:17:26.209 --> 00:17:31.500
By 1890, it had become the second-largest
engine manufacturer in Germany and was poised

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:37.370
to grow even more after the addition of two
new partners - Friedrich von Fischer and Julius

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:38.370
Ganss.

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:42.370
As for Karl, he cared about the one thing
he always cared about - the engineering.

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:47.440
Throughout the 1890s, he came up and patented
several new innovations, most notably the

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:52.679
double-pivot steering, planetary gear transmission,
and the flat engine with a boxer configuration

00:17:52.679 --> 00:17:56.480
which was better known as the contra engine
back then.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:18:00.510
At the recommendation of his partners, Karl
designed a new and improved automobile - the

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:03.340
Benz Victoria which went on sale in 1892.

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:07.870
However, it was his follow-up design, the
Benz Velo, that really took off and came to

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:11.450
be regarded as the world’s first large-scale
production car.

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:16.029
By the end of its sales run in 1902, over
1,200 Velos had been sold.

00:18:16.029 --> 00:18:20.279
And, while Karl was looking for new ways to
use his increasingly-powerful engines, he

00:18:20.279 --> 00:18:31.139
also invented the motorized bus, originally
capable of seating eight people.

00:18:31.139 --> 00:18:35.990
Although Benz &amp; Co was successful, it was
feeling the heat from its number one competitor,

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:42.259
Daimler Motoren Gesellschaft (DMG) headed
by Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach.

00:18:42.259 --> 00:18:47.250
Daimler passed away in 1900 but, just one
year later, Maybach designed and produced

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:50.690
his crowning achievement - the Mercedes 35
HP.

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:54.930
While previous cars still mostly looked like
coaches with engines fitted to them, this

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:59.279
was a vehicle we would consider to resemble
our modern idea of a car.

00:18:59.279 --> 00:19:02.929
Not to mention that it was the first automobile
branded as a Mercedes.

00:19:02.929 --> 00:19:07.419
And in case you were wondering where the Mercedes
name comes from, it was named after the daughter

00:19:07.419 --> 00:19:13.500
of Austrian businessman Emil Jellinek who
not only commissioned the 35 HP, but was very

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:18.100
hands-on in its development and specifically
requested certain improvements that ensured

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:21.240
the car became a landmark moment in automotive
history.

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:25.590
To counter their competition, the Benz company
brought in some French designers to help with

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:26.590
new models.

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:31.179
This angered Karl Benz who resigned his position
in 1903, although he still remained on the

00:19:31.179 --> 00:19:32.279
board of directors.

00:19:32.279 --> 00:19:37.440
His sons Richard and Eugen soon followed suit
and they founded a new company, Carl Benz

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:41.460
Söhne, in 1906, headquartered in Ladenburg.

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:45.690
They searched for new markets and found success
in England where their cars became quite popular

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:47.389
as taxis.

00:19:47.389 --> 00:19:51.710
Because the Benz family had enough capital
by this point, they never issued public stocks

00:19:51.710 --> 00:19:55.100
in this new company so they could retain complete
control over it.

00:19:55.100 --> 00:20:00.070
Benz Sons lasted until 1923, although Karl
Benz liquidated his shares and retired in

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:03.379
1912, leaving the business in the hands of
his sons.

00:20:03.379 --> 00:20:07.910
During the 1920s, the German economy plummeted
and all industries were hurting, including

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:08.970
the automotive one.

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:14.870
In order to survive, Benz &amp; Co and DMG signed
an “Agreement of Mutual Interest” in 1924.

00:20:14.870 --> 00:20:18.850
The automakers combined their production and
marketing efforts while still retaining their

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:22.030
individual brands in order to save on costs.

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:26.279
This did not last long as just two years later,
they merged completely and formed Daimler-Benz.

00:20:26.279 --> 00:20:30.990
Immediately, this deal was a success as their
sales tripled in 1927.

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:38.840
All their new vehicles were labeled as Mercedes-Benz,
a brand that is still as popular as ever.

00:20:38.840 --> 00:20:41.450
Karl stayed on the board of directors for
the rest of his life.

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:46.889
He died on April 4, 1929, at the age of 84,
in his home in Ladenburg.

00:20:46.889 --> 00:20:51.500
Bertha kept on living in the same home, until
she also passed away on May 5, 1944.

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:55.820
They both lived long enough to see the industry
that they helped create and dedicated their

00:20:55.820 --> 00:21:06.200
entire lives to grow and thrive into one which
became indispensable

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:12.059
to humanity.

